    "slot_key": "Approval override blocking until extra approval",
    "slot_value": "The system should block the action until the required second approval or documented exception is recorded, unless an agreed emergency setting applies; users should still be able to save the request as pending and notify the approver right away, then retry after approval without re-entering everything as long as the content and target account have not changed. If the content or target account changes before approval, the pending request should be invalidated rather than silently updated, because the approval was based on a specific version. The user should resubmit so the approver is reviewing the current state, and the system should show what changed and why the old request no longer applies. Minor non-material changes, like a typo fix, may be handled as a fresh review rule, but the preference is to be conservative. A request should be invalidated if a later change affects what was approved in a meaningful way, including the text, media, destination account, audience targeting, schedule, or any compliance-sensitive setting. Minor non-substantive edits that do not change the message or destination can continue without a fresh review, but when in doubt the preference is to require a new review, especially for regulated or high-risk accounts. Material fields are the ones that can change the meaning, reach, timing, or compliance risk of the post, such as text content, images or video, target account, audience, schedule, and campaign assignment. Cosmetic fields are spelling fixes, formatting, or other edits that don’t alter the message or destination. In a middle case where a request is limited to one business unit but touches a sensitive content class or expands approval rights, it should still go to central review; local teams can make the business case and recommend it, but central should make the final call. Central may approve it as a temporary exception with an expiration and audit record.",
