[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-4-2-1",
    "slot_key": "Keyword tracking scope and alerting behavior",
    "slot_value": "Monitor keywords to spot brand mentions, campaign performance, and customer issues early so the team can respond quickly and adjust content; surface the main response activity on each channel, keyword hits tied to the campaign, and highlight unusual patterns like a surge in mentions, a negative comment pattern, or a missed scheduled post. Routine noise should be grouped or suppressed, while important alerts should be escalated to the right role, especially if they affect an active campaign or a high-priority account. Users can review trends or comments, but only managers or approvers can adjust monitoring rules or mark items as resolved when there’s a customer or reputation issue. Operationally, unauthorized or unmanaged items are skipped entirely and should not notify anyone beyond maybe an internal error log; duplicate or too-late items can still be ingested for history but should skip alerting. Late or duplicate items may be kept for audit trail or manager review of patterns later, but they should not reopen the normal response workflow unless someone manually flags them.",
    "operation": "refine"
  },
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-4-2-2",
    "slot_key": "Campaign history retention and search capabilities",
    "slot_value": "Use campaign history to review trends or comments, and preserve past campaigns, published posts, and comments or replies if they matter for reference so the team can pick up work without losing context; also keep a clear history of what was posted, approved, changed, and when it went live. Access should remain limited day to day to the brands or campaigns each person is assigned to, and users should not change historical records. Retention should mostly follow the client’s contract and any legal or compliance rules, since some customers will want a longer history for reporting and others won’t. Audit record access should be restricted to the same roles that can see the related brand or campaign, with admins or compliance users only if explicitly needed for oversight. If content is private, unauthorized, or otherwise sensitive, it should be masked or limited as much as possible while still keeping enough detail for the audit trail.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

