    "slot_value": "One person drafts the content or response, another person reviews it, and someone with publishing rights sends it out; for campaigns, a contributor prepares the materials, an approver checks brand and compliance, and a publisher schedules or posts it; customer responses follow the same handoff so a message can be assigned, reviewed if needed, and answered without losing track of who did what. If content is rejected or flagged for a policy issue, it must not go out as-is; the team should be able to see exactly why it was blocked; the contributor and approver must be notified; a manager should be able to review it if there’s any question or an exception is possible; the item should stay in draft or needs revision status until it is fixed and sent back through approval. If publishing succeeds but a related piece like replies or ingestion fails, keep the core campaign running and mark the affected part as degraded rather than stopping everything; retry automatically for transient issues; escalate immediately if the failure persists, affects multiple accounts, or blocks a role needed for approval or publishing. An approval override is allowed without escalation only when the person has explicit authority for that account or campaign type, such as a designated publisher or manager acting within assigned limits. If the override goes beyond their authority, it requires a second approval from someone higher up or a documented exception process with a clear reason and timestamp, and the system should block the action until that extra approval is recorded unless an agreed emergency setting applies. When letting someone deviate from the standard campaign path, the system should also check whether the required approvals are missing, and if the deviation would bypass a required review it should block or require very explicit confirmation.",
